WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.470
- So you've been pretty
open about your hearing loss

00:00:03.470 --> 00:00:06.090
and tell everybody that
doesn't know, what happened?

00:00:06.090 --> 00:00:08.370
- I've been diagnosed with a
thing called Meniere's disease

00:00:08.370 --> 00:00:10.585
and I was diagnosed like thirty years ago.

00:00:10.585 --> 00:00:13.903
I lost my right ear and
had mounts of vertigo

00:00:13.903 --> 00:00:18.490
and lost my hearing in my right
ear about thirty years ago.

00:00:18.490 --> 00:00:20.720
I lost eighty percent of it.

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:23.175
And I lived, existed on
one ear, for you know,

00:00:23.175 --> 00:00:24.920
most of this time.

00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:27.163
Then two years ago, two years and a month,

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:29.761
I lost my left ear.

00:00:29.761 --> 00:00:30.594
(audience shock)

00:00:30.594 --> 00:00:33.620
- And now although it
fluctuates occasionally

00:00:33.620 --> 00:00:34.920
which gives me hope.

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:35.847
- Yeah.

00:00:35.847 --> 00:00:39.330
- It's really bad most of the time

00:00:39.330 --> 00:00:41.022
and the worst part is
that I can't hear music.

00:00:41.022 --> 00:00:42.334
- As a musician, oh

00:00:42.334 --> 00:00:46.640
- And music is so much harder
to listen to than speech.

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:48.700
I can hear speech a little
bit with hearing aids

00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:49.660
I wear hearing aids
- Mhm

00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:50.493
- And all that.

00:00:50.493 --> 00:00:52.031
And I could Bluetooth
through my phone and all that

00:00:52.031 --> 00:00:56.920
but music, the real problem
is that my left side,

00:00:56.920 --> 00:00:59.630
the lower frequencies
just fall of the cliff

00:00:59.630 --> 00:01:01.541
and I have residence or distortion.

00:01:01.541 --> 00:01:02.374
- [Kelly} Mhm.

00:01:02.374 --> 00:01:04.187
So, I can't hear a base part that would go

00:01:04.187 --> 00:01:06.590
(bass noise)

00:01:06.590 --> 00:01:07.698
it goes

00:01:07.698 --> 00:01:09.828
(loud, distorted, bass noise)

00:01:09.828 --> 00:01:10.661
- [Kelly] Oh, man.

00:01:10.661 --> 00:01:11.620
- [Guest] Like it's a blown speaker

00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:12.870
- Yeah, like it's distorted.

00:01:12.870 --> 00:01:15.795
- I went to a famous EMT
guy, when I first had it

00:01:15.795 --> 00:01:18.527
And he said well, he looks at me and says

00:01:18.527 --> 00:01:19.868
"Well, get use to it."

00:01:19.868 --> 00:01:21.430
when I lost the one ear.

00:01:21.430 --> 00:01:23.909
- Well that's some, bed side table manner.

00:01:23.909 --> 00:01:24.816
I'm like wow.

00:01:24.816 --> 00:01:25.649
(laughing)

00:01:25.649 --> 00:01:26.482
- I said you are kidding me

00:01:26.482 --> 00:01:27.700
I said I'm a musician, I'm worried about

00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:30.430
he says look, "Brian Wilson had one ear,

00:01:30.430 --> 00:01:32.120
Jimmy Hendricks had one ear."

00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:32.953
The guy said,

00:01:32.953 --> 00:01:36.200
"I have one ear and I've been
in a barber shop quartet."

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:37.073
I said really?

00:01:37.073 --> 00:01:39.450
- I love that, you
probably needed that guy,

00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:40.960
you needed that guy in that moment.

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:41.793
- I did.

00:01:41.793 --> 00:01:43.040
- Yeah, just so it's like okay,

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:44.070
I'm going to crawl out of this.

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:45.270
This is not what I wanted.

00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:47.747
- Not good for mixing
records, stereo, y'know...

00:01:47.747 --> 00:01:49.435
- Yeah, yeah.
(failed stereo sounds)

00:01:49.435 --> 00:01:50.268
There's no stereo.

00:01:50.268 --> 00:01:51.204
- You only got the one.

00:01:51.204 --> 00:01:53.113
(laughing)

